init_radio_config_default's sibling VAP-default function (same file) sets
private_ssid_2g/5g's default security.mode to wifi_security_mode_wpa3_personal
(SAE/WPA3) whenever _PLATFORM_BANANAPI_R4_ is defined -- true for this
container too, but mac80211_hwsim rejects it.

Root-caused via a raw netlink capture (strace -x on the exact
NL80211_CMD_START_AP/NEW_BEACON sendmsg for wifi0, decoded attribute-by-
attribute against linux/nl80211.h's enum nl80211_attrs): the request carries
NL80211_ATTR_SAE_PWE (attribute 298), which hostapd's driver_nl80211.c only
ever sends when wpa_key_mgmt_sae(key_mgmt_suites) is true (see
nl80211_put_sae_pwe's call site, source/drivers/driver_nl80211.c). The kernel
ACKs the whole START_AP request with a plain -EOPNOTSUPP (no extended-ack
text) as soon as that attribute is present -- confirmed this is genuinely
SAE-specific, not the 802.11ax/be/spectrum-mgmt defaults already disabled
under HWSIM_RADIO (0004 here, 0006 in rdk-wifi-hal): the failure persisted
identically after those, and the SSID/beacon-interval/DTIM/cipher-suite/IE
attributes decoded around it are all unremarkable, standard values.

Gated behind the same HWSIM_RADIO macro as this recipe's other hwsim-only
default overrides, falling back to the existing non-BananaPi-R4 branch
(WPA2-PSK, no SAE) -- real BananaPi R4 hardware is unaffected.

Capability gate (review P1 #3): 6 GHz mandates SAE-H2E + PMF, so the blanket
"HWSIM_RADIO => WPA2" override is wrong once wifi2 participates. On a 7.0 host
SAE works on hwsim (proven, doc/easymesh/Linux-7.0-hwsim-6GHz-VLP-AP-results.md),
so the override is additionally gated on !defined(HWSIM_6GHZ_CAPABLE): the WPA2
forcing applies only when HWSIM_RADIO && !HWSIM_6GHZ_CAPABLE. Building with
-DHWSIM_6GHZ_CAPABLE (the same flag that keeps 6 GHz enabled, patch 0006)
restores the WPA3/SAE/MFP-required defaults so a capable host can bring up a
standards-correct tri-band VAP. Default (flag off) is byte-for-byte the old
behaviour. A finer band-scoped split (WPA2 on 2.4/5, SAE on 6) is a possible
future refinement; capability-gating already satisfies the 6 GHz requirement.

